WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.560
SPEAKER: Identity and
Access Management,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.990
or IAM, in Google
Cloud, gives you

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.220
fine-grained control over the
permissions that users have.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.480
Today, we're going to
look at group membership

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.660
and the resource hierarchy to
understand how you can allow

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.310
users to access resources.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.890
Let's start on the
IAM page, where

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.973
we can see the permissions
for the different users

00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:22.950
on a project.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.000
In our project named,
My Project, the user,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
user@tsc.joonix.net, has been
granted the Storage Object

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.300
Viewer role.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.110
This role allows a user to
list and download objects

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.330
from storage
buckets, but does not

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.750
allow for creation or
deletion of any objects.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.570
This role was granted to
the user in the Projects IAM

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.350
policy.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.680
So it'll apply to the whole
project and all buckets

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:47.723
in the project.

00:00:47.723 --> 00:00:49.140
Let's take a look
at those buckets

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.940
by going to the
cloud storage page.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.300
If our user were to go into
the your_bucket_321 bucket,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.940
they'll be able to view all
objects because of the storage

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.200
viewer role at
the project level.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.519
As we said before, they won't
be able to upload a new object

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.930
or delete an existing object.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.680
The same behavior can be
observed on the command line.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.760
If the user runs gsutil ls,
all the objects from the bucket

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.020
are listed.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.680
When the user tries deleting
an object with gsutil rm,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.870
the operation fails
with an error message

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.020
that the user does not have
the storage.objects.delete

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.070
permission.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.970
So far, so good.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.530
Let's take a look at another
bucket named my_bucket_321.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.600
Here, our user seems
to have options

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.150
to delete and upload objects.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:39.270
We can see this by
running gsutil rm

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.120
and seeing that the
operation succeeds.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.730
What would cause this difference
in behavior between the two

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:45.570
buckets?

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.250
Here, we can see
that both buckets

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.940
belong to the same project.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.300
So our user should have the
same Storage Object Viewer role.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.930
The user should have received
a permission denied error when

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.700
trying to delete the object.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.440
To investigate
this kind of issue,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.300
the organization's
security admin

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.000
can use the IAM
policy troubleshooter.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.550
Let's head to the IAM and
Admin page and then click

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.590
on Policy Troubleshooter.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.389
Here, you'll be
able to see details

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:12.030
on what permissions a user has.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.490
We can put in our user's
email, the full resource

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.550
path, and the permission
we want to investigate.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.580
If you want to see more details
about Policy Troubleshooter,

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.710
check out the documentation.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.080
After we click
Check API Call, we

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.900
can see that the user has
the Storage Object Admin

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.850
role at the bucket
level due to being

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.090
a part of the
group@tsc.joonix.net group.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.600
Storage Object Admin lets users
do more than just view objects.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.240
They can also create
and delete them.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.100
Even though the user
has the viewer role

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.190
on all buckets in the
project, having the admin role

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.800
on this specific
bucket overrides that.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.900
In order to use the
policy troubleshooter,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.940
you'll need to have the
ability to see policies

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.080
across the organization, such as
with the iam.security reviewer

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.200
role.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.110
You also need access to
see group memberships

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.045
for any groups that you
want to list users for.

00:03:01.045 --> 00:03:02.670
Check out the
documentation to see more

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.190
about using the Policy
Troubleshooter and which

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.540
roles you'll need.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

